<h1>The Complete Tattoo Aftercare Guide</h1>
<p class="muted">How to heal a new tattoo so the lines stay crisp and the color stays bold — the full timeline, day by day.</p>

<p>A fresh tattoo is, medically speaking, a shallow open wound with ink in it. The tattooing is only half the work; the other half is the next two to four weeks, and it belongs to you. Good healing protects the artwork your artist made. Poor healing — picking, soaking, sunburn, or smothering it in ointment — can blur lines, drop color out of the skin, and in the worst cases invite infection. The good news is that proper aftercare is simple. It is mostly about keeping the tattoo clean, lightly moisturized, and left alone. This guide follows the same advice tattoo artists give their own clients, in the order you will need it. Your own artist's instructions always win if they differ — they saw your skin and know what they used — but if you have lost the aftercare sheet, this is the routine to follow. None of this is medical advice; if something looks wrong, skip to the warning signs section and call a professional.</p>

<h2>The first 24 hours</h2>
<p>Your artist will finish by washing the tattoo and covering it. That cover is either a traditional wrap (cling film or an absorbent pad, usually removed within a few hours) or a transparent adhesive film often called second skin, which can stay on for several days. Leave the cover on for exactly as long as your artist said — no longer, no shorter. When it comes off, wash your hands first, then wash the tattoo gently with lukewarm water and a fragrance-free, antibacterial soap, using only your fingertips. Do not use a washcloth, sponge, or anything abrasive. Pat it dry with a clean paper towel — bath towels harbor bacteria — and let it air-dry for a few minutes. Then apply a very thin layer of aftercare balm or ointment, just enough to make the skin look slightly moisturized, not greasy. If it looks shiny and wet, you used too much; blot the excess. Tattooed skin still needs to breathe.</p>

<h2>Days 2–7: the wash-and-moisturize routine</h2>
<p>For the first week, wash the tattoo once or twice a day and moisturize two to three times a day with that same thin layer. Expect the area to be sore, a little swollen, and warm for the first couple of days — that is normal inflammation, not infection. If your artist used second-skin film, some fluid will collect under it and make the tattoo look blurry or soupy through the plastic. That is also normal. Take the film off when your artist said to (or early if it leaks, peels to expose the tattoo, or the skin around it turns angry red), remove it slowly under warm running water, then start the wash-and-moisturize routine. Sleep in clean, loose clothing — fresh tattoos can weep a little plasma the first night and stick to sheets or tight fabric. If fabric does stick, soak it loose with warm water; never rip it off.</p>

<h2>Weeks 2–3: the peel and the itch</h2>
<p>Somewhere around day four to seven, the top layer of skin starts to flake and peel, like a sunburn. Colored flakes are normal — you are not losing the tattoo; the ink sits deeper than the layer that sheds. This phase also itches, sometimes intensely. Do not scratch, and do not pick or pull at flakes or any small scabs. Picking pulls ink out of skin that has not finished healing and is the single most common cause of patchy, faded spots that later need touch-ups. A thin layer of moisturizer calms most itching. Slapping the area lightly — a trick every tattooed person learns — works surprisingly well. By the end of week three the surface usually looks healed: smooth, slightly shiny new skin over the design. The deeper layers are still settling, so keep treating it gently for another week or two.</p>

<h2>The rules that protect the ink</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No soaking.</strong> Showers are fine; baths, swimming pools, hot tubs, lakes, and the ocean are off-limits until the tattoo is fully healed — at least two weeks, three is safer. Soaking softens scabs and invites bacteria.</li>
  <li><strong>No direct sun.</strong> Fresh tattoos sunburn fast and UV fades pigment. Keep it covered with clothing while it heals. After it has healed, use high-SPF sunscreen on it every time you are out — sun protection is the difference between a tattoo that stays bold for decades and one that washes out in five summers.</li>
  <li><strong>No gym shortcuts.</strong> Light exercise is fine, but avoid workouts that rub, stretch, or sweat heavily through the tattooed area for the first week, and wipe down any shared equipment before it touches your skin — or better, keep the tattoo covered.</li>
  <li><strong>No fragrance, no alcohol, no exfoliants.</strong> Scented lotions, scrubs, and harsh soaps irritate healing skin. Plain and fragrance-free is the whole game until it heals.</li>
  <li><strong>Do not re-wrap it</strong> in cling film after the first cover comes off, and do not let anyone — friend, partner, or pet — touch it with unwashed hands.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Warning signs: when to get help</h2>
<p>Normal healing includes soreness for a few days, mild swelling, peeling, and itching. It does <em>not</em> include spreading redness that grows after the third day, red streaks running away from the tattoo, increasing pain instead of decreasing, yellow or green discharge, a bad smell, bumps filled with pus, or fever. Any of those can mean infection, and infections in skin need a doctor or urgent-care visit promptly — not more ointment. Allergic reactions to specific pigments (most often reds) show up as persistent raised, itchy, or scaly patches confined to one color; those are worth showing to a dermatologist. When in doubt, message your artist a photo first — good artists want to know — but do not wait on a reply if symptoms are escalating.</p>

<h2>Long-term care</h2>
<p>Once healed, a tattoo needs almost nothing: moisturize when the skin is dry, and use sunscreen. Colors stay saturated when skin stays healthy. If a small spot heals light because a scab came off early, ask your artist about a touch-up — most artists offer one free or cheap within the first months, and it is a five-minute fix compared to living with a gap. And when you are planning the next piece, bring your healed photos to the consultation: how your skin holds ink tells your artist a lot about how to draw for you.</p>

